{"name":"Red Ochre","hex":"#953334","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red ochre","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A claylike material containing ferric oxide, used as a pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red_ochre"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ochre","blocks":[{"text":"Ochre is a family of natural clay earth pigments, made up of ferric oxide and varying amounts of clay and sand, ranging in colour from yellow to deep orange or brown. The term is also used for the colours produced by this pigment, especially a light brownish-yellow. A variant of ochre containing a large amount of hematite, or dehydrated iron oxide, has a reddish tint known as red ochre. The term \"ochre\" is colloquially used to cover a range of different substances used for their colours in Aboriginal Australian art and ceremonial decoration, for instance the clay known as \"white ochre\" or \"pipe clay\" is derived from kaolin or gypsum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG/330px-Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ochre","wikidata":"Q12353401"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Goldocker-_Pigment.JPG/500px-Goldocker-_Pigment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Goldocker-_Pigment.JPG/1280px-Goldocker-_Pigment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Goldocker-_Pigment.JPG/500px-Goldocker-_Pigment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yellow ochre (Goldochre) pigment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoldocker-_Pigment.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Lascaux2.jpg/500px-Lascaux2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Lascaux2.jpg/500px-Lascaux2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image of a horse coloured with yellow ochre (17,300 BC) from Lascaux cave, France","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALascaux2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Pech_Merle_main.jpg/500px-Pech_Merle_main.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Pech_Merle_main.jpg/500px-Pech_Merle_main.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image of a human hand created with red ochre in Pech Merle cave, France (Gravettian era, 25,000 BC)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APech_Merle_main.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Tomb_of_Nakht.jpg/500px-Tomb_of_Nakht.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/th